<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Amazing Community – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Amazing Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This School Has Been Here a Long Time!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Simplify complex instructions for classmates.
concept: Use diagrams to explain concepts to peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify complex instructions for classmates, using visuals (e.g., draw pictures on a map to indicate the type of building).
Can use diagrams to simplify community topics for peers (e.g., draw a map of the community and label main areas).
Can explain facts about familiar roles in the community using simple diagrams and words (e.g., shows a drawing of a classroom and says, 'Teachers in our school help students learn.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different community members (e.g., a teacher, a farmer, a police officer, a carpenter) and asks, "What do they do?" and "How long have they worked in our community or school?" Students briefly share their ideas. (e.g., "A teacher helps students learn at school. Maybe for many years!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to simplify a short sentence using a diagram. The teacher writes "The school librarian, Mrs. Ana, has worked here for 25 years. She organizes all the books." Then, the teacher draws a simple stick figure of a librarian, writes "Mrs. Ana, librarian," and "25 years here" with an arrow pointing to a book. The teacher explains, "I want to explain to a friend about Mrs. Ana. I can draw a picture to make it easy to understand!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I explain something difficult to make it easy for my friends?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I use drawings or pictures to help explain better?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simplified example of a "Community Interview Project" summary on a large chart or whiteboard. This includes a short paragraph about a community member and simple interview questions. For example:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"Our friend, Mr. Carlos, is a farmer in our community. He grows fresh vegetables. He has worked on his farm for 30 years. People like his delicious food at the market every Saturday."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Below it, a simple diagram showing: a farmer (Mr. Carlos), vegetables, "30 years," and "market."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Discuss community roles: 'What does your neighbor do?' Share community activities: 'Our community has a festival each year.' Ask about experiences: 'How long has your teacher worked here?'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher asks gist questions about the example: "Who is Mr. Carlos?" "What is his job?" "How long has he worked as a farmer?" "What does he sell at the market?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice simplifying information about community roles, using present simple, past simple, and present perfect (e.g., 'How long has...?', 'I have worked...'), and target vocabulary (e.g., 'teacher', 'farmer', 'cook', 'build', 'community', 'market'). Emphasis on pronunciation of compound nouns like 'neighborhood' and short/long vowel sounds in words like 'cook' and 'school'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: Simplify the Sentence:</b> The teacher provides a few slightly complex sentences about community members or instructions for the "Community Interview Project" on the board. Students work individually to rewrite them in a simpler way, focusing on key information.
                        <br><i>Example:</i> "Before you visit a community member for the interview, you need to prepare questions like 'How long have you worked here?' and 'What does your job involve?'"
                        <br><i>Simplified:</i> "First, ask questions. 'How long have you worked here?' 'What does your job do?'"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: Diagram & Explain:</b> The teacher gives students slips of paper with different community roles or activities (e.g., 'A police officer helps the neighborhood stay safe.', 'A musician plays at the festival every year.', 'Our school library has been here for 70 years!'). In pairs, students choose one slip, draw a simple diagram to represent the information, and then practice explaining it simply to their partner. They should use target grammar like "She has worked..." or "It has been here...".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: Explain Your Project Idea:</b> Students brainstorm what community member they might interview for their "Community Interview Project." In pairs, they describe their chosen person's job and responsibilities to their partner, attempting to simplify it and thinking about what visuals they could use. They practice asking "What does your neighbor do?" and "How long has she worked there?" about their imagined interview subject.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Our Community Interview Project: Simple Explanation"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group imagines they need to explain their "Community Interview Project" to a new student who just arrived at their school. They create a simple poster or large drawing that visually summarizes: (1) who they plan to interview or have interviewed, (2) what that person's job is, (3) how long they have done that job, and (4) one interesting fact about them. They should use simple words and diagrams/pictures on their poster. They practice explaining their project plan using the target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "We will interview a police officer. She protects our neighborhood. She has worked for ten years!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in mediating information about community roles and the project using simple language and visuals. Collaborative interaction within the group to create and explain their simplified project. They should use questions like 'What does your neighbor do?' and 'How long has your teacher worked here?' but adapted to their interview subject.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on clarity of explanation, appropriate use of visuals, and use of target grammar/vocabulary. Peer assessment by listening to another group's presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their "Community Interview Project: Simple Explanation" poster/diagram to the class (or another group). They explain their project plan or findings using their simplified visuals, ensuring their explanation is clear and easy for classmates to understand.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and simplicity of the mediated explanation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of visuals/diagrams to simplify the information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of present simple, past simple, and present perfect (e.g., 'She has worked for...', 'He cooks delicious food').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of vocabulary, including stress in compound nouns (e.g., 'neighborhood').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative effort within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I make my project explanation easy to understand for my classmates today?" (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using drawings or pictures help me explain my ideas?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the easiest part of explaining your project simply with pictures?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of simplifying information for your classmates was difficult, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students mediate information and use visuals? Were they able to use present perfect for experiences effectively?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks, perhaps with more complex information or different types of visuals?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This mediation lesson solidifies understanding of community roles and the "Community Interview Project." The next theme could build on these findings, perhaps exploring community challenges and solutions, where students would need to mediate information about problems and suggest simple ideas for change.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>